      "id": "7636841316634168589",
      "url": "https://www.tiktok.com/@thestomachdoc/video/7636841316634168589",
      "author_handle": "thestomachdoc",
      "author_followers": 2100000,
      "caption": "If your poop feels sharp, you could have something called an anal fissure — which is a small cut in your anal canal — or possibly a painful hemorrhoid.\n\nAnd when that happens, the goal is simple:\n\nYou need to make your poop softer, smoother, and easier to pass.\n\nThat usually means treating constipation, and increasing both types of fiber:\n\nSoluble fiber pulls water into the stool and helps create a softer, gel-like poop that is easier to pass. Good examples include oats, chia seeds, psyllium husk, beans, lentils, apples, and berries.\n\nInsoluble fiber adds bulk and helps move stool through the gut more efficiently. You’ll find it in vegetables, whole grains, bran, nuts, seeds, and leafy greens.\n\nIf your stool is hard, dry, or painful, pushing harder can make hemorrhoids worse and keep an anal fissure from healing.\n\nSo if you’re dealing with sharp poop pain, don’t ignore it. Start by softening the stool, increasing fiber slowly, drinking enough water, and get checked if there’s bleeding, severe pain, or symptoms that don’t improve.\n\nSave this if you struggle with constipation, hemorrhoids, or painful poop.\n\n#poop #constipation #fiber #hemorrhoids",
